hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1078","**\nNarrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa‘id, narrated to us Sa‘id ibn Abi ‘Arubah, and likewise narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Ibn Abi ‘Adiyy, from Sa‘id; and likewise narrated to us Harun ibn Ishaq, narrated to us ‘Abdah, from Sa‘id; and likewise narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Mu‘adh ibn Hisham, narrated to me my father Hisham, all of them from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Sa‘d ibn Hisham—and this is the hadith of Yahya ibn Sa‘id—that he divorced his wife and then came to Madinah to sell some property he owned there in order to purchase weapons and armor and to perform jihad against the Romans until he died.\n\nHe met a group from his people who told him that a group from his people had sought to do the same during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Prophet (ﷺ) said, “Does there not exist in me an excellent model?” and forbade them from doing so. He then bore witness to the reconciliation with his wife.\n\nHe returned to us and informed us that he had met Ibn ‘Abbas and asked him about the witr prayer. Ibn ‘Abbas said, “Shall I not inform you of the most knowledgeable person on earth regarding the witr prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?” He said, “Yes.” Ibn ‘Abbas said, “It is ‘A’ishah. Go to her and ask her, then return to me and inform me of her reply.”\n\nSo he went to Hakim ibn Aflah and sought his intercession to her. Hakim said, “I will not approach her. I forbade her from speaking about these two sects, but she insisted on proceeding with them. I swore an oath to her, so he came with me and entered upon her.”\n\nShe said, “Hakim?” and recognized him. He said, “Yes,” or “Indeed.” She said, “Who is this with you?” He said, “Sa‘d ibn Hisham.” She said, “Whose son is Hisham?” He said, “Ibn ‘Amir.” She invoked mercy upon him and said, “What an excellent man ‘Amir was!”\n\nI said, “O Mother of the Believers, inform me about the witr prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).”\n\nShe said, “We would prepare for him his siwak and his purification water, and Allah would awaken him for whatever He willed from the night. He would then use the siwak, perform wudu, and pray eight rak‘ahs without sitting except at the eighth rak‘ah. He would sit, remember Allah, and supplicate.”\n\nHarun added in his narration at this point, “Then he would stand without saying the salam, then pray the ninth rak‘ah. He would sit, praise his Lord and send blessings upon His Prophet (ﷺ), then say the salam audibly such that we could hear him. Then he would pray two rak‘ahs while sitting, making a total of eleven rak‘ahs, O my son.”\n\nBundar and Harun both said, “When he grew older and gained flesh, he would pray witr with seven rak‘ahs and then pray two rak‘ahs while sitting after the salam, making a total of nine rak‘ahs, O my son.”\n\nBundar said in the narration of Ibn Abi ‘Adiyy from Sa‘id from Qatadah: “And he would say the salam once audibly such that we could hear him.”\n\nBundar said: I said to Yahya, “The people say ‘once’ (taslimatan).” He said, “This is how I memorized it from Sa‘id,” and likewise Harun said in his narration from ‘Abdah from Sa‘id: “Then he would say the salam audibly such that we could hear him,” just as Yahya said.\n\n‘Abd al-Samad said, from Hisham, from Qatadah in this report: “Then he would say the salam once audibly such that we could hear him.”"
"1079","Likewise, Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Hisham, H narrated to us, and Ali bin Sahl al-Ramli narrated to us, Ma’mal bin Ismail narrated to us, Amara bin Zadan narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us, on the authority of Anas, who said: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, “used to pray the Witr prayer with nine rak’ahs, but when it became old and heavy He performed the Witr prayer by seven, and prayed two rak’ahs while sitting, reciting in them “By the Most Gracious” and “Al-Waqi’a.” Anas said: And we recite “with short surahs when they shake,” and “Say, O you disbelievers,” and the like."
